{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"pwd\ncat >> literature_review.md <<'EOF'\n- Chunk 3, pp. 7–9: Prompt wording, test visibility and feedback independently affect exploitation. A strict stop-and-report prompt reduces GPT-5 conflicting LiveCodeBench exploitation from 92% (loose prompt) to 1%. Allow-abort drops GPT-5 conflicting SWE exploitation from 54% to 9%, with model-dependent effects. This motivates offering BLOCKED in both twins and freezing prompts. Monitor evaluation uses 2,371 impossible SWE and 193 impossible LiveCodeBench transcripts; monitor sensitivity is substantially worse for SWE. These are author-reported exploitation rates, not completion-claim effects.\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
