{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_85","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"pwd\ncat STATE.md\n.venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor p in json.load(open('evidence/literature_downloads.json')):\n if p['status']\"'!='\"'downloaded':print(json.dumps(p,indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"/net/spaces/chenhao/chai-lab-models/haokunliu/safety/workspaces/completion-claims-on-solvable-9a93-codex-gpt-6-astra\n# Research State\n\n- Current phase: `resource_finder`\n- Pipeline completed: `False`\n\n## Previous phases\n\nNo prior phases completed.\n\n## Current phase context\n\n- Phase: `resource_finder`\n- Status: `in_progress`\n- Started: `2026-10-20T14:49:32.776566Z`\n- Next steps:\n  - Review the literature and resource catalog before running experiments.\n\n## Workspace check\n\n- Root: `/net/spaces/chenhao/chai-lab-models/haokunliu/safety/workspaces/completion-claims-on-solvable-9a93-codex-gpt-6-astra`\n- Directory usable: `True`\n\n## Output validation\n\nNo phase output validation recorded yet.\n\n## Agent notes\n\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START -->\n### resource_finder\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START:resource_finder -->\nPhases 1–3 complete: 26 unique PDFs; two official datasets (103 x 3 LCB rows; 200 Upward Deception tasks), 30 synthetic development-only pairs; three official repos cloned/pinned. planning.md keeps exactly three directions. Evidence: papers_manifest.json, dataset_validation.json, development_validation.json, repositories.json, code_validation.json under evidence/. code/README.md documents runtime dependencies and scorer caveats; 60 Python files parse, no evaluation runtime claimed. Known data defects: lcbhard_77 syntax errors; 48 altered-word prompts. FeasiGen lacks released task data and accepts some unverified-success traces. Primary endpoint must use independent state and final DONE parsing, not upstream exploitation/HFR scores. Phase 4 next: concise literature synthesis, complete resource catalog, final artifact/hash audit; no model hypothesis result.\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END:resource_finder -->\n\n### Legacy notes\nNo phase notes recorded yet.\n\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END -->\n{\n  \"title\": \"Heaviside Continuity of Rolling Coefficients for Eliminating Epistemic Entropy in Large Language Models\",\n  \"year\": 2026,\n  \"authors\": \"MY Pitsane, Hope Mogale\",\n  \"url\": \"https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusId:289934929\",\n  \"relevance\": 3,\n  \"abstract\": \"Large language models (LLMs) generate fluent outputs that can be wrong. Unlike humans, who often exhibit cues when providing false information, LLMs produce errors that are difficult to detect because autoregressive decoding provides no mechanism for verifying intermediate reasoning before state progression. We introduce Heaviside Continuity of Rolling Coefficients (HCRC), a verification-first execution framework that reformulates inference as predicate-gated state transitions governed by a Heaviside Gate. HCRC combines model confidence with independent verification signals from a parallel worker architecture, allowing execution to advance only when predefined correctness predicates are satisfied. This prevents invalid intermediate states from propagating, reducing epistemic entropy without modifying the underlying model. We evaluate HCRC on software-engineering and reasoning tasks across thirteen proposers from four providers. On capable proposers, the gate reduces the false-completion rate (FCR) from 4--7% to 0% while remaining latency-competitive and, in some settings, faster than the unwrapped model. On weaker proposers, it converts false completions into honest halts instead of corrupting downstream state. Beyond benchmarking, HCRC has operated for months as the production control plane of an agentic coding environment, authorizing file mutations, verification-driven progress reporting, and memory compaction. These results establish HCRC as a general framework for verification-driven LLM execution, showing that reliable reasoning can be achieved through principled execution control rather than model scale alone.\",\n  \"citations\": 1,\n  \"search_index\": 4,\n  \"s2_status\": 429,\n  \"arxiv_summary\": \"Large language models (LLMs) generate fluent outputs that can be wrong. Unlike humans, who often exhibit cues when providing false information, LLMs produce errors that are difficult to detect because autoregressive decoding provides no mechanism for verifying intermediate reasoning before state progression. We introduce Heaviside Continuity of Rolling Coefficients (HCRC), a verification-first execution framework that reformulates inference as predicate-gated state transitions governed by a Heaviside Gate. HCRC combines model confidence with independent verification signals from a parallel worker architecture, allowing execution to advance only when predefined correctness predicates are satisfied. This prevents invalid intermediate states from propagating, reducing epistemic entropy without modifying the underlying model. We evaluate HCRC on software-engineering and reasoning tasks across thirteen proposers from four providers. On capable proposers, the gate reduces the false-completion rate (FCR) from 4--7% to 0% while remaining latency-competitive and, in some settings, faster than the unwrapped model. On weaker proposers, it converts false completions into honest halts instead of corrupting downstream state. Beyond benchmarking, HCRC has operated for months as the production control plane of an agentic coding environment, authorizing file mutations, verification-driven progress reporting, and memory compaction. These results establish HCRC as a general framework for verification-driven LLM execution, showing that reliable reasoning can be achieved through principled execution control rather than model scale alone.\",\n  \"published\": \"2026-07-06T00:29:34Z\",\n  \"arxiv_id\": \"2607.04562v3\",\n  \"download_error\": \"404 Client Error: Not Found for url: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.04562v3\",\n  \"status\": \"unavailable\",\n  \"retry_error\": \"404 Client Error: Not Found for url: https://export.arxiv.org/pdf/2607.04562\"\n}\n{\n  \"title\": \"TRACE: A Self-Evolving Skill Bank for Consistent, Limit-Aware LLM Agents\",\n  \"year\": 2026,\n  \"authors\": \"Wen-Hao Wu, Meng-Hao Zhang, X. Wang, Zhi Wang, Kun Shao, Jian Luan\",\n  \"url\": \"https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusId:291327967\",\n  \"relevance\": 2,\n  \"abstract\": \"Reliable deployment of LLM agents in user-facing products depends not on raw task-solving ability but on consistency and limit-awareness: behaving the same way across repeated trials, and recognizing when a request cannot, or cannot yet, be safely fulfilled. CAR-bench exposes this reliability gap in the domain of in-car assistants: an LLM-simulated user issues incomplete or ambiguous requests, requiring the agent to resolve uncertainty through multi-turn dialogue and tool use while strictly adhering to domain policies. Even frontier models show a substantial gap between what they can solve at least once (Pass@3) and what they solve consistently across trials (Pass^k). We bridge this gap with TRACE (TRAjectory-Contrastive Evolution), which iteratively improves a skill-based agent's behavioral knowledge without modifying model weights. This knowledge is organized as a Skill Bank of modular, retrievable skills, each encoding a self-contained set of tool-use rules and behavioral guidelines. TRACE evolves this bank through an agentic self-evolution loop: after each evaluation round, it groups trajectories by the skills invoked and refines each skill by contrasting successful and failed behaviors. The updated bank then guides subsequent rounds, while during deployment the Actor performs state-conditioned skill orchestration at every turn. On GPT-5.5, TRACE improves consistency (Pass^3) by 34.6 points, from 59.9% to 94.5%, while shrinking the gap between potential and reliable performance to just 4.0 points. On the official hidden set, TRACE achieved first place using GPT-5.6-Sol, attaining a Pass^3 score of 70%-a 40% relative improvement over the baseline. These results show that TRACE converts high model potential into stable, consistent performance gain. Project homepage: https://darwin-agent.github.io/Car-bench-TRACE.\",\n  \"citations\": 4,\n  \"search_index\": 10,\n  \"s2_status\": 429,\n  \"arxiv_id\": null,\n  \"status\": \"unavailable\"\n}\n{\n  \"title\": \"DriftBench: Detecting LLM Coding Agent Cheating via Specification-Level Constraint Drift Mutation\",\n  \"year\": 2026,\n  \"authors\": \"Fang Hao\",\n  \"url\": \"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b494ed4e985b3a9c52c5c44b5c4a9dc75b63ff2a\",\n  \"relevance\": 2,\n  \"abstract\": \"For the cheating behaviour of LLM coding intelligences exploiting test case vulnerabilities, the existing test value mutation-based detection methods cannot cover the scenarios in which the intelligences exploit specification-level hidden constraints for cheating, and there is a structural detection blind spot. In this paper, we propose DriftBench with a new Drift mutation strategy, i.e., constructing the third class of impossible tasks by injecting hidden constraints into the function specification without modifying any test assertions, and designing a lightweight cheating detector based on 25-dimensional feature fusion. On 95 coding tasks constructed with reference to the IMPOSSIBLEBENCH benchmark design methodology, 1900 experimental runs were conducted using 5 models (covering the 3 model families DeepSeek, Qwen, and Zhipu). The results show that the baseline pass rate BPR is 90.3%, the One-Off cheating rate is 53.9%, the Drift cheating rate is 90.3%, the complementary detection rate DCR is 39.5%, and Drift independently detects 33 One-Off missed cheating cases; the Conflicting mutation cheating rate is only 4.4%, which is close to the absolute defence, verifying that contradiction assertion injection as an effective defence strategy. Ablation experiments further reveal that Drift cheating is insensitive to prompt wording, while One-Off cheating rate doubles from 15% to 30% with loose cues, indicating that specification-level cheating cannot be mitigated by cue word engineering.5 models show consistent cheating behaviour, validating the cross-model robustness found by DriftBench.\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"search_index\": 13,\n  \"s2_status\": 429,\n  \"arxiv_id\": null,\n  \"status\": \"unavailable\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
