ts Know What They Can't Do? Evaluating Feasibility Awareness in Tool-Using Agents\n2606.09863v1 From Confident Closing to Silent Failure: Characterizing False Success in LLM Agents\n2606.23175v1 Position: Correct Answer, Wrong Mechanism - When AI Scientists Defend General Claims Their Own Data Contradicts\n2607.13596v2 Protective Capacity Hallucination: When Large Language Models Claim Nonexistent Capabilities\n2607.19449v1 Guardrails as Scapegoats: Auditing Unfaithful Safety Refusals in Tool-Augmented LLM Agents\n2609.03267v1 Refusing the Impossible: A Taxonomy and Benchmark for Code Hallucination in Large Language Models\n2609.14758v1 Fabrication After Tool Failure: Tool-Augmented Agents Assert Values Their Tools Did Not Return\n2609.15494v3 The Troy Moment: How LLM Agents Adjudicate the Decision Point Under Impossible Tasks, Claimed Authority, and Peer Information\n2609.20812v3 Quantifying Overclaiming Propensity in Frontier LLM Agents\n2609.35732v1 Failure-Transparent Agents: Benchmarking Post-Failure Reporting in Tool-Using Language Models\n2609.38411v1 A Competing-Hazards Systematization of Loss of Control in Autonomous Agents\n2610.02001v1 Mingbird: A Local-First Agent Harness Enabling Small Open Models to Complete Real Tasks\n2610.07274v1 A Trust Layer for Agent Evaluation\n2610.19226v1 Agentic Self-Awareness: Aligning Knowing, Acting, and Holding\n2608.22793 TRACE: A Self-Evolving Skill Bank for Consistent, Limit-Aware LLM Agents\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
